Subject: [PATCH 11/36] Image studio: a cast, a composer and the parameters, in
 three columns
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The chat metaphor is wrong for this work. It hides the parameters behind
the prose, it makes history unbrowsable, and it has nowhere to put a
reference that accumulates over months. So: the cast on the left, the
composer and a grid of batches in the middle, the knobs on the right.

Every generation goes through the resolver and the resolved prompt is on
screen under a disclosure, with a numbered line for each decision — the
LoRA that was applied, the one that was refused because it belongs to
another family, the negative prompt a distilled model cannot steer with.
A control that cannot run stays visible and disabled carrying its reason;
hiding it would teach the writer the feature does not exist.

Which fields the parameters column offers is decided by the model and by
what the request can actually carry. That second half is a type: when the
runtime grows a sampler field, the table in operations.ts stops compiling
and says which line to change, instead of a comment quietly going stale.

Also here: compare two to four pictures with the recipe diff underneath,
iterate on a recipe without ever substituting a missing model, the chapters
each character appears in, and the training-set export with its captions
drafted from the codex entry.

